 creativity, autonomy through the work of trace settled soil, decanting and paper-filter operations by vessel id and producing a clear decanting and filtration process record. These are possible work priorities, not salary or job guarantees."}}, "embedding_text": "ROLE\nName: Water Cleaning Engineer\n\nCAREER CONTEXT\nIndustry: Water & Environmental Services\nDomain: Water & Environmental Engineering\nRole Family: Water Treatment Engineer\n\nROLE SUMMARY\nDesigns or improves methods for separating unwanted substances from water and checks whether treatment claims are supported. Future role: Water Treatment Engineer; school activities are introductory demonstrations.\n\nWORK CONTEXT\nA water-treatment engineering team examines how settling, decanting and filters work and whether equipment claims are supported. School experiments are illustrative rather than treatment-plant certification.\n\nTYPICAL WORK ACTIVITIES\nTrace settled soil, decanting and paper-filter operations by vessel ID; Compare suspended soil with dissolved salt; Link mass and volume when describing solution concentration; Examine whether the filter advertisement exceeds the evidence\n\nKEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACTIONS\nTransfer and Filter: decant from Jar A into Jar B without disturbing settled soil; pass the liquid through filter paper into Cup C; keep vessel IDs attached to readings; Calculate Salt Solution Concentration: identify mass and volume from the same mixture, apply the taught concentration expression and record the correct units; Inspect What Paper Filtration Retains: distinguish source solutions, observe what the paper retains, and document the filtrate without merging their samples; Evaluate the Filter Advertisement: quote the claim, compare it with supported filtration results, identify missing controls and write a bounded decision\n\nDELIVERABLES & OUTPUTS\nDecanting and filtration process record; Salt-solution concentration working; Separate soil and salt filtration log; Filter-claim assessment with evidence limits; Learning modules: RMR-CAP-L01-M02 - Transfer and Filter (Core, step 1), RMR-CAP-L04-M01 - Calculate Salt Solution Concentration (Important, step 2), RMR-CAP-L04-M02 - Inspect What Paper Filtration Retains (Important, step 3), RMR-CAP-L04-M04 - Evaluate the Filter Advertisement (Supporting, step 4)\n\nINTEREST AND WORK-STYLE ALIGNMENT\nRealistic work involves physical treatment processes and equipment; Investigative work checks measurements and limitations; Conventional discipline makes calculations and process records traceable.\n\nAPTITUDE ALIGNMENT\nKey areas: Numerical Reasoning, Logical Reasoning, Spatial Reasoning\nReadiness: Water Cleaning Engineer uses numerical reasoning, logical reasoning, spatial reasoning to trace settled soil, decanting and paper-filter operations by vessel id, compare suspended soil with dissolved salt, and interpret source-based records. These priorities are proposed role demands, not measured learner aptitude.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL ALIGNMENT\nKey traits: Openness, Conscientiousness, Emotional Stability\nWork style: Water Cleaning Engineer benefits from openness, conscientiousness, emotional stability when performing compare suspended soil with dissolved salt and following through on filter-claim assessment with evidence limits. Traits guide supportive coaching, not exclusion.\n\nWORK-VALUE ALIGNMENT\nKey values: Impact, Creativity, Autonomy\nMotivation: This pathway may reward impact, creativity, autonomy through the work of trace settled soil, decanting and paper-filter operations by vessel id and producing a clear decanting and filtration process record. These are possible work priorities, not salary or job guarantees.\n\nEDUCATION AND ELIGIBILITY\nGate: Mandatory\nStreams: B.Tech. Chemical Engineering; B.Tech. Environmental Engineering; B.Tech. Civil Engineering\n"}'::jsonb;
